Introduction
	This help section explains how to work with XFaceMaker
	to create C++ applications. Specifically, it explains:
	
	- how to create a C++ class with XFaceMaker,
	
	- how to call a C++ method from a FACE script.
	
Creating a C++ Class
	XFaceMaker can generate a C++ class based on an interface
	module that you wish to access as a C++ object.
	
	As for other modules, you must first create your model
	object for your class. The model can be any widget,
	composite or not.
	
	As opposed to other modules, C++ classes cannot be
	created and instanciated dynamically in XFaceMaker. For
	this reason, there is no command for loading a C++ class,
	and there is no way of creating an instance of a C++ class.
	All you can do is generate the C++ code for the class
	and instanciate it in a C++ application.
	
	When defining the model object for the C++ class, you can
	specify:
	
	- values of Motif/Xt resource: these will be set automatically
	when the C++ class instance is created;
	
	- sub-widgets: if the class model is a composite widget with
	sub-widgets, these will be created automatically in the instance;
	
	- C++ class members: to define class members, open the
	Active Values box, select the `C++ class' usage toggle, and
	enter the name, type, default value, and side-effects of the
	member; you can choose to define a data member (i.e. with associated
	storage in the class) or a method (without storage), and you
	can specify the access mode (public, private or protected);
	in the Set and Get scripts of data member, the value is passed
	as the @ argument of the script.
	
	Once your model object is defined, call the command `Module/C++ Class'.
	A special file selector pops up. You must select:
	
	- the name of the C++ class;
	
	- the style, i.e. the base class from which your C++ class
	will be derived; you can choose either the NSL style or the
	Rogue Wave style which is based on classes of the View.h++
	library by Rogue Wave Software;
	
	- the header file suffix;
	
	- the C++ file name where the class will be saved;
	
	- the .fm file where the model will be saved.
	
	See the documentation for more details.
	
Calling C++ methods
	When you are developing the user interface of a C++ application,
	it is useful to be able to call C++ methods of C++ classes
	defined or used by your application. The FACE interpreter/compiler
	provides a special syntax to do that.
	
	Let us assume that your application defines or uses a class name
	`Foo' which contains a method `int bar(char*)'. You must
	declare the method in a FACE script as follows:
	
	function Int "Foo::bar"(String);
	
	Then, you must declare a variable of type `Foo' and initialize it.
	We will assume that there is a function `GetFoo()' which returns
	a `Foo' object:
	
	Foo foo = GetFoo();
	
	Now, you can call the `bar' method as follows:
	
	i = foo->bar("hello");
	
	The FACE interpreter recognizes the bar method, and calls it
	with the implicit `this' object pointer passed as the first
	argument to the method.
	
	If you want your C++ application to run in interpreted mode
	(i.e. the FACE scripts are interpreted, not compiled into C code),
	you have to attach the method's address to the name "Foo::bar"
	as follows:
	
	FmAttachFunction("Foo::bar", (FaceFunctionPtr)Foo::bar, "Int", 1, "String");
	
	(Note: if you are using g++, use `(FaceFunctionPtr)&Foo::bar'
	instead).
	
	In compiled mode, the method call will be translated to
	a regular C++ method call `foo->bar'.
	
